 PART 4 - SERIALIZABLE isolation catches the race (no constraint needed)
========================================================================
Part 1 again on the unprotected table, but both sessions use SERIALIZABLE.
[  1.58s] Session A BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE; free? found = 0
[  1.58s] Session B BEGIN ISOLATION LEVEL SERIALIZABLE; free? found = 0
[  1.58s] Session A INSERT booking for Musa Danjuma
[  1.58s] Session B INSERT booking for Tobiloba Bakare
[  1.58s] Session A COMMIT ok
[  1.58s] Session B COMMIT -> ERROR 40001: ERROR:  could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions
[  1.58s] Session B retry: free? found = 1 -> bed taken, show B another bed

Result: 1 booking kept; the second one was refused with 40001

========================================================================
 PART 5 - READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ
========================================================================

--- Session A at READ COMMITTED ---
[  1.59s] Session A BEGIN; next-session bookings in this lodge = 19
[  1.60s] Session B INSERT tenancy 54962 in the same lodge; COMMIT
[  1.60s] Session A same query again, same transaction  = 20

--- Session A at REPEATABLE READ ---
[  1.61s] Session A BEGIN; next-session bookings in this lodge = 19
[  1.61s] Session B INSERT tenancy 54963 in the same lodge; COMMIT
[  1.61s] Session A same query again, same transaction  = 19

Result: READ COMMITTED saw B's new row mid-transaction (non-repeatable read);
REPEATABLE READ kept the snapshot from its first query.

========================================================================
 PART 6 - DEADLOCK, and the fix (lock rows in one fixed order)
========================================================================
Two pending instalments: 2 and 4. A finance clerk (A) and the payment
webhook (B) each update both, in OPPOSITE order. Everything is rolled back.
[  1.62s] Session A BEGIN; UPDATE instalment 2  (A locks 2)
[  1.63s] Session B BEGIN; UPDATE instalment 4  (B locks 4)
[  2.13s] Session A UPDATE instalment 4 ... waiting for B
[  2.13s] Session B UPDATE instalment 2 ... waiting for A  -> each waits for the other
[  2.63s] Session A ERROR 40P01: ERROR:  deadlock detected
[  2.63s] Session B UPDATE finished (the other session was chosen as the deadlock victim)
[  2.63s] both      ROLLBACK

--- Fix: both sessions lock the two rows in ascending id order first ---
[  2.64s] Session A BEGIN; lock 2,4 in id order -> got both
[  3.14s] Session B lock in id order ... waits politely (no cycle possible)
[  3.14s] Session A UPDATE 4 and 2; ROLLBACK (demo only, releases the locks)
[  3.15s] Session B got the locks, UPDATE both -> done, no deadlock

